\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/run/current-system/sw/bin/bash -lc 'find loops -maxdepth 3 -type f | sort'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/run/current-system/sw/bin/bash -lc 'find loops -maxdepth 3 -type f | sort'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"find: ‘loops’: No such file or directory\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"The repo has no `loops/` directory in the working tree, so I’m pulling the relevant context from `spec.md`, the codebase, and the loop checkpoint commits instead.\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/run/current-system/sw/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,240p' spec.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/run/current-system/sw/bin/bash -lc \\\"sed -n '1,240p' spec.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\\n# Aquarium / Planted Tank Ecosystem Simulator — Implementation Spec (Rust)\\n\\nVersion: 0.1  \\nTarget system: freshwater planted shrimp tank (`Neocaridina davidi`)  \\nPrimary UI: terminal / text UI  \\nCore requirement: scientifically grounded, deterministic, agent-implementable\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Objective\\n\\nBuild a Rust simulation engine that models a living freshwater planted aquarium as an ecosystem rather than a scripted set of events.\\n\\nThe simulator must let a player:\\n\\n- configure tanks of different sizes and shapes\\n- stock and maintain a tank over time\\n- change husbandry variables, including **ambient temperature**\\n- observe chemistry, plant growth, algae, biofilm, microfauna, shrimp condition, and long-term stabilization\\n- encounter realistic emergent problems such as:\\n  - cycling delays\\n  - ammonia or nitrite stress\\n  - algae blooms\\n  - oxygen dips\\n  - breeding success/failure\\n  - molt issues\\n  - detritus accumulation\\n  - instability in small tanks compared with larger tanks\\n\\nThe design must be specific enough that an implementation agent can build the system incrementally without re-architecting the codebase.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Scope\\n\\n## 2.1 In scope for v1\\n\\nA single freshwater tank containing:\\n\\n- water column\\n- tank geometry and water volume\\n- substrate\\n- filter / biomedia compartment\\n- heater (optional but supported)\\n- light\\n- rooted plants\\n- periphyton / biofilm\\n- free algae\\n- microbial guilds\\n- microfauna guilds\\n- shrimp population\\n- detritus / dissolved organics\\n- player maintenance actions\\n\\n## 2.2 Out of scope for v1\\n\\n- photorealistic graphics\\n- saltwater / reef chemistry\\n- disease-specific pathology models\\n- species-level microbial taxonomic simulation\\n- exact CFD, hydrodynamics, or molecular chemistry\\n- multiplayer\\n- online features\\n\\n## 2.3 Planned later\\n\\n- fish populations\\n- snails and other macro-grazers\\n- additional plant archetypes\\n- scenario editor\\n- richer rendering frontend\\n- more detailed carbonate chemistry and ion pairing\\n- external research calibration tools\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Scientific modeling stance\\n\\nThe simulation is **mechanistic and guild-based**.\\n\\nIt must not attempt molecule-level or species-complete ecological simulation. Instead, it must model the main causal pathways that drive real aquarium outcomes:\\n\\n- waste generation -> ammonia -> nitrite -> nitrate\\n- pH and temperature effects on unionized ammonia risk\\n- oxygen and carbon cycling\\n- plant vs algae competition\\n- substrate and biofilm effects\\n- microfauna grazing and predation loops\\n- shrimp feeding, stress, molting, and reproduction\\n- tank-size-dependent stability\\n\\nImportant scientific anchors for the model:\\n\\n- Unionized ammonia fraction increases with pH, and temperature affects ammonia toxicity.\\n- Home aquarium biofilters show real microbial succession and variable cycle times rather than one fixed “recipe.”\\n- Aquarium biofilters commonly include AOA and comammox `Nitrospira`, so nitrification should not be represented as instant or purely magical conversion.\\n- Submerged macrophytes can take nutrients from sediment and/or the water column.\\n- Rising temperature interacts with nutrient availability and can shift the plant/algae balance.\\n- Protozoan grazing affects bacterial communities and nutrient mineralization.\\n- `Neocaridina davidi` reproduction is temperature-sensitive; high temperatures can suppress ovarian maturation and spawning.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Design principles\\n\\n1. **Deterministic core**\\n   - same seed + same actions + same version = same result\\n\\n2. **Mass-balance first**\\n   - store masses/amounts internally where possible\\n   - derive concentrations for display and toxicity calculations\\n\\n3. **Tank size matters**\\n   - volume, footprint, depth, surface area, and wall area must affect the dynamics\\n\\n4. **Engine/UI separation**\\n   - core simulation crate must not depend on terminal or graphics code\\n\\n5. **Data-driven parameters**\\n   - species and process parameters loaded from data files\\n   - each important parameter carries provenance metadata\\n\\n6. **Progressive fidelity**\\n   - start with robust abstractions\\n   - refine later where science or gameplay demands it\\n\\n7. **Visible causality**\\n   - user should be able to inspect why the tank is improving or failing\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Functional requirements\\n\\nThe system MUST support:\\n\\n- different tank dimensions and fill heights\\n- different substrate types and depths\\n- at least one shrimp species pack\\n- at least two plant guilds\\n- at least two algae/biofilm guilds\\n- nutrient and oxygen cycling\\n- water temperature dynamics influenced by ambient temperature\\n- partial water changes with configurable source water\\n- feeding and detritus accumulation\\n- reproduction and juvenile survival for shrimp\\n- time acceleration (hours, days, weeks)\\n- logging of events and causes\\n- deterministic save/load\\n\\nThe system SHOULD support:\\n\\n- seasonal ambient temperature schedules\\n- heater thermostat behavior\\n- filter cleaning side effects\\n- top-off water\\n- fertilizer dosing\\n- photoperiod editing\\n- different light strengths\\n- simple denitrification in oxygen-poor substrate microzones\\n\\nThe system MAY support later:\\n\\n- fish\\n- snails\\n- disease\\n- graphical rendering\\n- multiplayer or shared scenarios\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Tank size and geometry model\\n\\nTank size is a first-class simulation input, not cosmetic metadata.\\n\\n## 6.1 Required geometry inputs\\n\\n```rust\\npub struct TankGeometry {\\n    pub length_cm: f32,\\n    pub width_cm: f32,\\n    pub height_cm: f32,\\n    pub fill_height_cm: f32,\\n    pub glass_thickness_mm: f32,\\n    pub open_top: bool,\\n}\\n```\\n\\n## 6.2 Derived geometry quantities\\n\\nThe engine MUST derive:\\n\\n- `water_volume_l`\\n- `surface_area_cm2` = length * width\\n- `footprint_area_cm2`\\n- `wall_area_cm2`\\n- `substrate_plan_area_cm2`\\n- `mean_depth_cm`\\n- `surface_area_to_volume_ratio`\\n- `wall_area_to_volume_ratio`\\n\\n## 6.3 Why geometry matters\\n\\nThese derived values must affect simulation outcomes:\\n\\n- **Dilution / concentration**\\n  - same mass of feed in a 10 L tank causes a larger concentration spike than in 100 L\\n\\n- **Thermal inertia**\\n  - smaller tanks change temperature faster\\n\\n- **Heat exchange**\\n  - surface area and wall area affect passive exchange with ambient air\\n\\n- **Gas exchange**\\n  - open water surface area affects oxygenation / CO2 exchange\\n\\n- **Light distribution**\\n  - deeper tanks attenuate light more strongly with depth\\n\\n- **Plant carrying capacity**\\n  - footprint and substrate depth affect rooted plant biomass capacity\\n\\n- **Periphyton / biofilm area**\\n  - hardscape, glass area, and substrate expose colonizable surfaces\\n\\n- **Bioload stability**\\n  - small tanks are less forgiving; large tanks buffer fixed disturbances better\\n\\n## 6.4 Tank size classes\\n\\nThe engine must not hardcode classes, but example presets should exist:\\n\\n- nano: 5–20 L\\n- small: 20–60 L\\n- medium: 60–200 L\\n- large: 200+ L\\n\\nThe same scenario logic must run at any size.\\n\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/run/current-system/sw/bin/bash -lc \\\"rg -n \\\\\\\"loop 1|Loop 1|Workspace Skeleton|save/load|geometry|temperature|water change|ambient\\\\\\\" -S . --glob '\\\"'!target/**'\\\"'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/run/current-system/sw/bin/bash -lc \\\"rg -n \\\\\\\"loop 1|Loop 1|Workspace Skeleton|save/load|geometry|temperature|water change|ambient\\\\\\\" -S . --glob '\\\"'!target/**'\\\"'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"./spec.md:19:- change husbandry variables, including **ambient temperature**\\n./spec.md:42:- tank geometry and water volume\\n./spec.md:85:- pH and temperature effects on unionized ammonia risk\\n./spec.md:95:- Unionized ammonia fraction increases with pH, and temperature affects ammonia toxicity.\\n./spec.md:99:- Rising temperature interacts with nutrient availability and can shift the plant/algae balance.\\n./spec.md:101:- `Neocaridina davidi` reproduction is temperature-sensitive; high temperatures can suppress ovarian maturation and spawning.\\n./spec.md:143:- water temperature dynamics influenced by ambient temperature\\n./spec.md:144:- partial water changes with configurable source water\\n./spec.md:149:- deterministic save/load\\n./spec.md:153:- seasonal ambient temperature schedules\\n./spec.md:172:## 6. Tank size and geometry model\\n./spec.md:176:## 6.1 Required geometry inputs\\n./spec.md:189:## 6.2 Derived geometry quantities\\n./spec.md:202:## 6.3 Why geometry matters\\n./spec.md:210:  - smaller tanks change temperature faster\\n./spec.md:213:  - surface area and wall area affect passive exchange with ambient air\\n./spec.md:260:- temperature\\n./spec.md:285:- seasonal ambient patterns\\n./spec.md:306:    pub geometry: TankGeometry,\\n./spec.md:329:    pub ambient_temp_c: f32,\\n./spec.md:330:    pub ambient_temp_schedule: AmbientSchedule,\\n./spec.md:336:### Ambient temperature action\\n./spec.md:337:Ambient temperature must be editable by the player at runtime. This represents room heating/cooling, seasonal weather, AC failure, or relocation of the tank.\\n./spec.md:340:- water temperature trends\\n./spec.md:345:- ammonia risk indirectly through temperature and pH-sensitive chemistry\\n./spec.md:486:Plants are modeled as guilds, not per-leaf geometry.\\n./spec.md:593:- temperature\\n./spec.md:615:Water temperature must be dynamic.\\n./spec.md:620:dT/dt = (Q_ambient + Q_heater + Q_light - Q_evap) / heat_capacity\\n./spec.md:626:- `Q_ambient` is based on temperature difference between ambient air and water and a geometry-dependent transfer coefficient\\n./spec.md:635:Q_ambient = UA_total * (T_ambient - T_water)\\n./spec.md:641:- small open tanks respond faster to ambient changes than large tanks\\n./spec.md:642:- heaters partially buffer ambient swings\\n./spec.md:643:- if ambient exceeds heater setpoint, heater cannot cool the tank\\n./spec.md:644:- high ambient temperature reduces oxygen saturation and can stress shrimp\\n./spec.md:662:- water changes\\n./spec.md:666:- water changes\\n./spec.md:670:- water changes\\n./spec.md:674:Nitrification must be biomass-limited, oxygen-limited, temperature-sensitive, and pH-sensitive.\\n./spec.md:702:- compute NH3 fraction from pH and temperature each tick\\n./spec.md:745:- DO saturation decreases as temperature rises\\n./spec.md:763:Then solve or approximate pH each tick from alkalinity + DIC + temperature.\\n./spec.md:769:- do not hardcode pH as constant if plants, nitrification, and water changes occur\\n./spec.md:790:- water temperature\\n./spec.md:827:- temperature\\n./spec.md:870:- temperature\\n./spec.md:909:- temperature\\n./spec.md:921:    f_temperature\\n./spec.md:936:- temperature is stressful\\n./spec.md:1003:- mixes in source water temperature and chemistry\\n./spec.md:1014:### Change ambient temperature\\n./spec.md:1015:- changes room environment, not instant water temperature\\n./spec.md:1028:3. update water volume (evaporation/top-off/water change)\\n./spec.md:1029:4. update temperature from ambient + heater + equipment\\n./spec.md:1031:6. compute gas solubility targets from temperature\\n./spec.md:1092:- warm ambient temperature reduced oxygen saturation\\n./spec.md:1163:- save/load schema\\n./spec.md:1257:- temperatures in °C\\n./spec.md:1268:- no temperature below freezing in normal freshwater scenarios unless explicitly allowed\\n./spec.md:1300:   - temperature\\n./spec.md:1316:   - water change\\n./spec.md:1319:   - change ambient temp\\n./spec.md:1347:- 10 L tank must change temperature faster after ambient temp jump\\n./spec.md:1349:## 20.4 Ambient temperature test\\n./spec.md:1352:- ambient raised to 31 °C\\n./spec.md:1378:At high sustained temperature, shrimp reproduction readiness and success must fall relative to moderate temperature conditions.\\n./spec.md:1381:A large water change with very different GH/KH/TDS must improve some waste metrics while potentially increasing stress.\\n./spec.md:1393:- save/load roundtrip test\\n./spec.md:1395:## Milestone 1 — tank geometry and temperature\\n./spec.md:1400:- temperature update system\\n./spec.md:1401:- ambient temperature action\\n./spec.md:1405:## Milestone 2 — dissolved pools and water changes\\n./spec.md:1409:- water change/top-off actions\\n./spec.md:1436:- high-temperature reproduction penalty\\n./spec.md:1444:- save/load support\\n./spec.md:1479:  - moderate temperature optimum\\n./spec.md:1508:2. **ambient temperature as a real action**\\n./spec.md:1509:3. **temperature-dependent water behavior**\\n./spec.md:1510:4. **pH/temperature-dependent ammonia risk**\\n./spec.md:1515:9. **deterministic save/load**\\n./spec.md:1526:1. Ammonia speciation and toxicity depend strongly on pH and temperature.\\n./spec.md:1530:5. Rising temperature interacts with nutrient regime and can shift aquatic plant performance and algal competition.\\n./spec.md:1533:8. `Neocaridina davidi` reproduction is inhibited at sufficiently high sustained temperature.\\n./spec.md:1542:2. Implement geometry derivation and unit tests.\\n./spec.md:1544:4. Implement water change mixing.\\n./spec.md:1545:5. Implement ambient temperature and heater tick.\\n./spec.md:1565:- feed it, change water, and alter ambient temperature\\n./spec.md:1580:- EPA CADDIS: **Ammonia** — ammonia occurs as NH3 + NH4+, higher pH favors the more toxic unionized NH3, and temperature also affects toxicity.\\n./spec.md:1584:- Zhang et al. 2020, **Interactive Effects of Rising Temperature and Nutrient Enrichment on Aquatic Plant Growth, Stoichiometry, and Palatability** — temperature and nutrient regime interact to change plant growth; external nutrient loading can suppress plants through algal competition.\\n./spec.md:1587:- Baliña et al. 2018, **Losing Reproduction: Effect of High Temperature on Female Biochemical Composition and Egg Quality in Neocaridina davidi** — high sustained temperature inhibited ovarian maturation and spawning at 33 °C relative to 28 °C.\\n./crates/tank_data/src/presets.rs:15:    pub temperature_c: f64,\\n./crates/tank_data/src/presets.rs:84:    pub ambient_temp_c: f64,\\n./crates/tank_data/data/scenarios/nano_cycle.toml:12:ambient_temp_c = 24.0\\n./crates/tank_data/data/scenarios/medium_planted.toml:12:ambient_temp_c = 24.0\\n./